--TEST--
`Core\IO::canonicalize` answers one resolution for every spelling of one file and is a fixed point over its own answer — the agreement `Core\Path::normalize` cannot reach — and it throws for a name that is not there, where `Core\IO::exists` answers `false` — spec § 14 *Resolution*, `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, not a value: the resolved path is the operating system's own
// spelling -- a drive letter and a verbatim prefix on Windows, a plain absolute
// path elsewhere -- so a case that printed it would pin a platform rather than
// the member. What is the same everywhere is that every spelling of one file
// resolves to the same answer, which is the one question this member exists to
// let a program ask.
//
// A literal has its `.` and `..` removed while compiling, so each spelling is
// joined onto this file's folder while the program runs and reaches the member
// as written. `Here::path` returns a literal resolved from this file's folder.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
string $dir = Here::path('.');
string $canonical = Core\IO::canonicalize("tree/one.txt");

array<string> $spellings = [
    "tree/one.txt",
    "./tree/one.txt",
    "tree/./one.txt",
    "tree/nested/../one.txt",
    "./tree/nested/../../tree/one.txt",
];

int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($spellings as string $spelling) {
    if (Core\IO::canonicalize(Core\Path::join($dir, $spelling)) == $canonical) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}
echo "spellings that agree: ", $agreed as string, " of ", Core\Arr::count($spellings) as string, "\n";

// A fixed point: the answer resolves to itself. This is what makes two of them
// comparable at all -- a resolution a program stores and resolves again later
// has not moved, so equality over the answers is equality over the files.
if (Core\IO::canonicalize($canonical) == $canonical) {
    echo "resolving the answer again: unchanged\n";
} else {
    echo "resolving the answer again: moved\n";
}

// Two different files never agree, which is the half that says the count above
// is not just every call answering the same thing.
if (Core\IO::canonicalize("tree/two.txt") == $canonical) {
    echo "a second file: agrees\n";
} else {
    echo "a second file: differs\n";
}

// Absence is not an answer here. `exists` says `false` for the same name in the
// same breath, and the difference is the member's contract rather than an
// accident: a resolution of a name that is not there would be a path the
// program could then compare against a real one.
if (Core\IO::exists("tree/gone.txt")) {
    echo "gone.txt exists\n";
} else {
    echo "gone.txt does not exist\n";
}
try {
    Core\IO::canonicalize("tree/gone.txt");
    echo "canonicalize answered for a missing name\n";
} catch (IOError $missing) {
    echo "canonicalize threw for a missing name\n";
}

// It resolves and it does not *prove*. A name that leaves the base resolves
// perfectly well -- it is a real file, and this member has no base to hold it
// against -- while `within` is the member that refuses it. Reaching for the
// resolver when the question was containment is the mistake `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
// exists to prevent, and this pair is what that mistake looks like.
string $escape = Core\IO::canonicalize(Core\Path::join($dir, "tree/nested/../../outside.txt"));
if ($escape == Core\IO::canonicalize("outside.txt")) {
    echo "canonicalize resolved a path that leaves the base\n";
}
try {
    Core\IO::within("tree", "nested/../../outside.txt");
    echo "within admitted it\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $left) {
    // The second argument is joined to `tree`, not to this file's folder, so
    // the refusal is the containment check's own.
    if (Core\Str::contains($left->message, "must stay inside the base")) {
        echo "within refused it\n";
    } else {
        echo "within threw something else: ", $left->message, "\n";
    }
}
--FILE tree/one.txt--
one
--FILE tree/two.txt--
two
--FILE tree/nested/three.txt--
three
--FILE outside.txt--
outside
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--EXPECT--
spellings that agree: 5 of 5
resolving the answer again: unchanged
a second file: differs
gone.txt does not exist
canonicalize threw for a missing name
canonicalize resolved a path that leaves the base
within refused it
